<div class="container">
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <main class="docs-body">

      <p>Three new packs are live: <strong>Ukrainian</strong>, <strong>Polish</strong> and <strong>Czech</strong>. Each one is
        a full pack, not a preview — an on-device dictionary with inflections, a
        frequency-banded cloze bank, and a voice. Lector now covers fourteen
        languages at that depth.</p>

      <p>The first one started with a complaint, and the complaint was correct.</p>

      <h2 id="ukrainian">Ukrainian is not a Russian preset</h2>

      <p>A Ukrainian learner was reading Ukrainian text under the Russian pack,
        because that was the closest thing available. Two things went wrong, and
        both are worth spelling out.</p>

      <p>First, the definitions were quietly wrong. The word <strong>так</strong> means <em>yes</em> in Ukrainian. In Russian
        it means <em>like that</em>. Neither reading is a typo or an edge case — they are
        both ordinary words, and a learner has no way to tell that the drawer is
        answering a question about a different language.</p>

      <p>Second, the reader was cutting words in half. Ukrainian spelling uses the
        apostrophe as a letter, inside the word: <strong>п'ять</strong> (five), <strong>м'ясо</strong> (meat), <strong>з'їзд</strong> (congress).
        Russian has no such thing, so the Russian pack treats an apostrophe as a
        boundary and splits those words into two fragments, neither of which is
        in any dictionary.</p>

      <p>The Ukrainian pack fixes both. The apostrophe joins the word rather than
        breaking it, so п'ять stays one tappable token. Sources in the wild
        disagree about which apostrophe character to type — straight, curly,
        modifier letter — so a lookup finds the same entry whichever one your
        text used. The alphabet includes ґ, є, і and ї, and the definitions are
        Ukrainian definitions.</p>

      <h2 id="polish">Polish</h2>

      <p>Polish inflects hard: seven cases across three genders, verb aspect
        pairs, and consonant alternations that change the stem rather than just
        the ending. <em>noga</em> becomes <em>nodze</em>. <em>miasto</em> becomes <em>mieście</em>. <em>pies</em> becomes <em>psowi</em>. No
        suffix-stripping rule reaches any of those, so the pack resolves them
        through a table of 1,137,470 inflected forms instead of guessing.</p>

      <p>Polish uses the apostrophe too, but for the opposite job: it attaches a
        Polish case ending to a foreign name, as in <em>Joyce'a</em> or <em>Kennedy'ego</em>. There, splitting is the right
        answer — it leaves <em>Joyce</em> on its own, where you can look it up. Same
        character, two packs, opposite rules.</p>

      <h2 id="czech">Czech</h2>

      <p>In Czech, vowel length carries meaning. <strong>byt</strong> is a flat. <strong>být</strong> is the verb <em>to be</em>. They differ by
        one accent, and a lookup that folded that accent away would answer the
        wrong question every time. The pack keys them as two separate words.</p>

      <p>The stem changes are as sharp as Polish's: <em>kůň</em> becomes <em>koňmi</em>, <em>město</em> becomes <em>městě</em>, <em>pes</em> becomes <em>psovi</em>.
        Czech also has a letter that is not a character — <em>ch</em> is a single letter
        of the alphabet for sorting, but two characters in text, which turns out
        to need no special handling at all.</p>

      <h2 id="depth">What a pack actually contains</h2>

      <p>A language pack is four things, and every pack has all four:</p>

      <table class="env-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>Ukrainian</th><th>Polish</th><th>Czech</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Dictionary entries</td><td>54,563</td><td>169,953</td><td>68,249</td></tr>
          <tr><td>English senses</td><td>83,546</td><td>270,331</td><td>88,200</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inflected forms</td><td>346,122</td><td>1,137,470</td><td>439,882</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cloze sentences</td><td>9,663</td><td>10,518</td><td>9,312</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dictionary coverage</td><td>96.2%</td><td>96.7%</td><td>94.8%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>The dictionary runs on your own instance, so no lookup leaves the
        machine. Definitions carry IPA on all three, which means tapping a word
        shows how to say it as well as what it means. Each pack has a voice too.</p>

      <p>That last row is the one worth explaining. Coverage is measured by taking
        the 5,000 most frequent words in the language and asking how many of them
        the dictionary can actually resolve. It is a build-time gate, not a
        marketing number: a pack that scores below 85% does not ship.</p>

      <h2 id="limits">Where they fall short</h2>

      <p>Coverage is not 100%, and it is more useful to say where the gaps are
        than to round the number up.</p>

      <p>Czech builds its superlatives with a prefix — <em>starý</em> becomes <em>nejstarší</em> — and negates verbs the same way.
        Wiktionary lists some of those prefixed forms and not others, so a Czech
        Wikipedia article resolved 90.3% of its distinct words for us, and the
        misses were mostly <em>nej-</em> forms. Prefix rules would lift that, and are on
        the list.</p>

      <p>Very colloquial writing is harder again. The opening of <em>Švejk</em> is written in Prague vernacular
        — <em>takovej</em> for <em>takový</em>, <em>svýho</em> for <em>svého</em> — and scored 88.5%. That is a deliberate literary
        choice by Hašek rather than a defect in the pack, but it is the kind of
        text where you will notice the gaps. Proper nouns are the other reliable
        miss, in every language.</p>

      <p>When the on-device dictionary has no answer, the lookup falls through to
        your configured translation agent, so you still get a definition. The
        drawer tells you which one answered.</p>

      <h2 id="next">What's next</h2>

      <p>Czech, Polish and Ukrainian sit together on purpose: once one Slavic pack
        works, the next one is mostly data. The same reasoning puts Hungarian,
        Swedish, Finnish and modern Greek within reach.</p>

      <p>If you want a language that is not here yet, say so — the Ukrainian pack
        exists because somebody did. Pick it on the <a href="/#notify">home page</a> or on <a href="/reference-data/#notify">reference data</a>,
        and it is the most direct way to move a language up the list.</p>

